#{[17592186518894 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 - \\pi\\right) \\left(y + \\pi\\right) - 11\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518895 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(y + \\pi\\right) + 1\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518896 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + 3 \\pi\\right) \\left(y + 3 \\pi\\right) + 5\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518898 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 - \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 3\\right) - 11\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518899 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(y + \\pi\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518900 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=6 x + 2\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518901 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=\\left(1 + 2 \\pi\\right) \\left(y - 2 \\pi\\right) + 12\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518902 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\pi \\\\ z &=\\left(1 + 2 \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 3\\right) + 12\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186518903 :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=- 8 x - 16\\end{aligned}\\right."] [17592186521554 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=3x(1-0.5x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-4y+4xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$"] [17592186521554 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2xy &=0 \\\\ -4y+4xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+4xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+4x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)(1-0.5(1))-2(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,0.75).\n$$"] [17592186521560 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=2x(1-0.05x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$"] [17592186521560 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3xy &=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.05x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(20,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.05(2))-3(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-6y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,0.6).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(20,0),\\,(2,0.6).\n$$"] [17592186522787 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{12\\lambda }+2B{e}^{12\\lambda }+6(6A\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }+B{e}^{12\\lambda }+12B\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+6(6A\\lambda +B+12B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+36A\\lambda +6B+72B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+8(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1+6\\lambda  \\\\ 1+36\\lambda  & 8+72\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (8+72\\lambda )-(1+6\\lambda )(1+36\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 48\\lambda +432{\\lambda }^{2}-(1+42\\lambda +216{\\lambda }^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 216{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda +1)(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}B=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=B,$ where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =B{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}A+\\frac{4}{3}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-4B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-4B{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/3}(x-4) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/3}(x-4).$"] [17592186523101 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=0 \\\\ x+4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent"] [17592186523112 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-2z &=-4 \\\\ x+3y &=-8 \\\\ x+y-4z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent independent\n- **II.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent"] [17592186523119 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-5 \\\\ -2x+6y &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent"] [17592186523120 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=0 \\\\ -2x-y-7z &=-2 \\\\ 2x+11z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent independent\n- **II.** The system is inconsistent\n- **III.** The system is consistent dependent"] [17592186523134 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=5 \\\\ x+2y+2z &=7 \\\\ x+3y+5z &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent"] [17592186523146 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=1 \\\\ 2x+5y &=7 \\\\ -2x-3y+5z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is inconsistent\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is consistent dependent"] [17592186523149 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=1 \\\\ 2x+4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is inconsistent\n- **III.** The system is consistent independent"] [17592186523165 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=4 \\\\ -x &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent"] [17592186523181 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y-3z &=5 \\\\ 2x-5y-9z &=10 \\\\ 2x-5y-9z &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is inconsistent\n- **III.** The system is consistent independent"] [17592186523190 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-3 \\\\ -2x-4y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent independent\n- **II.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent"] [17592186523996 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-2),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda (x-2) \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }+2 \\\\ \\lambda &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{(\\frac{5}{2}-2)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens downwards, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{9}{4}.$"]}
